def _graph_nodes():
    s = open(os.path.join(DATA, "graph.js"), encoding="utf-8").read()
    return json.loads(s[s.index("{"):s.rindex("}") + 1])["nodes"]


def _reaches(N, consumer, provider):
    """True if `consumer` imports `provider`, transitively (graph.js node ids)."""
    for x in (consumer, provider):
        if x not in N:
            raise SystemExit(f"gen_suite_data: {x} is not a node of graph.js (run scripts/gen_docs_graph.py?)")
    seen, st = set(), [consumer]
    while st:
        x = st.pop()
        if x == provider:
            return True
        if x in seen:
            continue
        seen.add(x)
        st += N[x]["imports"]
    return False


def complexity_data():
    N = _graph_nodes()
    for prov, cons in CX_NO_IMPORT:
        if _reaches(N, cons, prov):
            raise SystemExit(f"gen_suite_data: the text says no Lean import connects {prov} and {cons}, but one does")
    R = lambda rel, start, **kw: grab(rel, start, root="", **kw)
    TEX = "paper/Exact_Table_Algorithm_Model_and_Limitations.tex"
    from math import factorial, log2
    s3 = round(log2(55 * factorial(54) * 72 ** 54))
    assert s3 == 576, f"S(3) is 2^{s3}, the page says about 2^576"

    LB = {}   # upper-bound figure blocks
    LB["Profile"] = dict(kind="narrow", title="Profile reduction", role="Lean: transfer · prose: existence", summary=r"""
<p><strong>The claim.</strong> A table influences the recursion only through three integers: its arithmetic charge \(c\) and two scanner offsets \(a, b\), through \(\text{child}(w)=\lfloor w/k\rfloor+1+\max\bigl(a,\ (w \bmod k)+b\bigr)\). The search over programs then becomes a search over profiles.</p>
<p><strong>In Lean.</strong> <code>Profile</code> (charge, <code>peakA</code>, <code>peakB</code>) and the recurrence <code>childWidth</code> are <em>definitions</em>. The theorems are the transfer: two profile assignments with equal used peaks give equal child widths and width traces, and equal peaks with no larger charges cost no more, with no monotonicity assumption on the terminal cost.</p>
<p><strong>Not in Lean.</strong> That <em>every</em> complete legal table has such a three-integer profile. That is the prose package <code>proof_notes/profile</code>, whose own header reads “analytical proofs with exact deterministic witnesses; not independently reviewed or Lean formalized”. Which triples are realizable is not settled there either: it calls realizability “the uniform bottleneck”.</p>""",
        snippets=[R("lean/Model/Defs.lean", r"^structure Profile", until=r"w / k \+ 1 \+ max", doc=True, name="Profile, childWidth",
                    note="The three integers, and the recurrence they feed."),
                  R("lean/Model/Scanner/Planner.lean", r"^theorem planCost_le_of_used_peaks_and_charges", until=r":= by\s*$", doc=True,
                    note="The transfer, for any terminal function (no monotonicity). <code>childWidth_eq_of_peaks</code> and <code>widthTrace_eq_of_used_peaks</code> are its width-only forms."),
                  R("proof_notes/profile/PROFILE_THEOREMS.2026-10-15T140100.md", r"^Every complete legal table P has", until=r"max\(0,a-\(k-1\)\) <= b <= a", name="the prose claim",
                    note="The existence claim, from the prose package (<code>PROFILE_THEOREMS</code> §1). Not machine-checked.")])

    LB["Ceiling"] = dict(kind="lean", title="Charge ceiling 27k³", role="Lean · unconditional", summary=r"""
<p><strong>The claim.</strong> Every feasible width cap has a minimum-charge witness of charge at most \(27k^3\). This is what makes the search finite, and it is used by the exact-optimality theorem.</p>
<p><strong>In Lean, unconditionally:</strong> for every \(k\ge2\), every \(K\) and every feasible cap, with no hypothesis on a baseline (<code>feasible_has_ceiling_grouped</code>, <code>minCharge_le_27_cube</code>). The proof goes through the repository's <em>grouped literal</em> baseline, whose charge is exactly \(4(k-1)(2k-3)\le 8k^2\) (<code>groupedLiteralBaseline_charge</code>).</p>
<p><strong>Not in Lean.</strong> The paper's <em>paired</em> constructor, with charge \(\le 6k^2\): only the count of its instruction skeleton is checked (<code>PairedCharge</code>); its legality is a paper proof that has not been independently reviewed. And \(27k^3\) is a sufficient search bound, not a prediction of the optimal charge (the paper notes 91,125 at \(k=15\)).</p>""",
        snippets=[R("lean/Provider/Ceiling/FinalCeiling.lean", r"^theorem minCharge_le_27_cube", until=r":=\s*$", name="minCharge_le_27_cube"),
                  R("lean/Provider/Ceiling/FinalCeiling.lean", r"^theorem feasible_has_ceiling_grouped", until=r":= by\s*$", name="feasible_has_ceiling_grouped"),
                  R("lean/Provider/Ceiling/GroupedCharge.lean", r"^theorem groupedLiteralBaseline_charge", until=r":= by\s*$", name="groupedLiteralBaseline_charge",
                    note="Exactly the repository baseline's charge. The paired constructor is a different object.")])

    LB["Exact"] = dict(kind="narrow", title="Exact optimality", role="Lean · fixed (K, W, N)", summary=r"""
<p><strong>The claim.</strong> For every \(K\ge2\), root width \(W\) and multiplicity \(N\), a policy that is correct on all signed \(W\)-bit inputs and costs no more than any ladder of source-legal literal-F2 tables of arity \(\le K\), one table per arity (programs of any length, on the fixed Kahanamoku-Meyer–Yao points).</p>
<p><strong>What the Lean statement is.</strong> A <em>pointwise</em> statement per \((K,W,N)\): the policy comes from a finite minimum-profile <em>provider</em> that performs an exhaustive search per key. It proves the optimum exists and is attained. It does <strong>not</strong> say that the paper's fixed-parameter algorithm computes it, and says nothing about running time.</p>
<p><code>pp_through_arity_K</code> is the weaker, catalogue-relative form: optimal only among a catalogue of verified candidates. Both depend only on <code>propext</code>, <code>Classical.choice</code>, <code>Quot.sound</code> (<code>scripts/AxiomAudit.lean</code>).</p>""",
        snippets=[R("lean/Provider/Effective/ProfileExact.lean", r"^theorem optimal_raw_F2_exact", until=r"≤ v\) := by", doc=True, name="optimal_raw_F2_exact"),
                  R("lean/PhaseProduct/Catalog/EndToEnd.lean", r"^theorem pp_through_arity_K", until=r"other\.benchmarkCost tauRS 1\) := by", doc=True, name="pp_through_arity_K",
                    note="The weaker form: <em>relative to the catalogue</em> of verified candidates.")])

    LB["OuterIlp"] = dict(kind="prose", title="Outer problem as an integer program", role="prose proof · Kannan imported", summary=r"""
<p><strong>The claim.</strong> With the cheapest table of each arity fixed, choosing the recursion is an integer program with one integer unknown per distinct arity used; the widths are affine expressions in those offsets, not extra variables. Kannan's exact integer-feasibility algorithm solves it in \(t^{O(t)}\,\mathrm{poly}(\text{input length})\), and \(t!\le U\) makes that polynomial in the root-stop cost \(U\).</p>
<p><strong>In Lean, only the surroundings.</strong> The affine width step (<code>step_divisor_residue</code>), the count of unknowns (<code>variable_count</code>: one per used arity) and <code>small_integer_point</code>. The equivalence of the optimum with integer-program feasibility, and Kannan's theorem itself, have no Lean statement. Kannan's theorem is an <em>external citation</em> (Math. Oper. Res. 12(3), 1987, Theorem 5.4); the scan is not shipped.</p>
<p>The proof is prose in <code>proof_notes/outer</code>, reviewed by AI reviewers, not machine-checked. The bound is for \(n\) in unary; it is not a claim in \(\log n\).</p>""",
        snippets=[R("lean/ILPEncoding/Labels/ILPChecked.lean", r"^theorem variable_count", n=2, name="variable_count",
                    note="One unknown per used arity. This is all Lean says about the integer program's shape."),
                  R("lean/ILPEncoding/Affine/Core.lean", r"^theorem step_divisor_residue", until=r":= by\s*$", name="step_divisor_residue"),
                  R(TEX, r"^The outer proof imports Kannan", until=r"expressions, not extra variables", name="paper, §6 (outer search)",
                    note="The paper's statement of what is imported."),
                  R("proof_notes/outer/KANNAN_APPLICABILITY.2026-10-15.md", r"^Theorem 5\.4 bounds", n=1, name="Kannan Theorem 5.4, as read",
                    note="Primary-source check of the imported statement (a scan with no text layer, read visually).")])

